window.docContents[236].resolve({"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.Process/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___Process":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › Process.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 6 headline theorems depend on it 19 of 70 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\n\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 38–44): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.SignalSession.ProcessResult.exited, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.ProcessResult.aborted, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.ProcessResult\n\ndef processInputsL50–68 ↗#The inputs of one run: the argument vector argv (argv[0] included, ASCII), the bytes stdin on file descriptor 0, the regular files files and the directories dirs that exist at the start, with paths relative to the process's working directory (the party's). The stack is 8 MiB, Linux's default RLIMIT_STACK, instead of the engine's default 128 KiB (DEV-CLI7). Every other field is the engine's default (ProcessInputs); execfn is [], so AT_EXECFN points to \"\" (Linux passes the execve path); the only reader is musl's __init_libc, which uses it for __progname only when argv[0] is null, and argv[0] is always \"libsignal-cli\" here (and __progname is never read); auxv.hwcap is 0x1b (FP bit 0, ASIMD bit 1, AES bit 3, and PMULL bit 4, so both aes 0.9.1's and SPQR's AT_HWCAP feature probes select ARMv8 hardware AES and PMULL GF(2¹⁶) while LSE bit 8 remains 0); in particular auxv keeps AuxvConfig's non-setuid ids and the loader sets AT_SECURE = 0 (DEV-CLI6).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/Process.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.Process/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___Process"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.SmDir/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___SmDir":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › SmDir.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 4 headline theorems depend on it 4 of 128 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nFunctional specification of the signal-session CLI in SM modeTrusted in part. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use partyAddress and textBytes; the remaining SM-mode directory and model definitions (dirOfE, smInputs, smSendInputs, smRecvInputs, smWrites, smPreKeyWire, smSendWire, smSendModel, smRecvModel) are checked by Lean.In the ACD19 secure-messaging setting (spqrOffSessionParams C), two parties A and B start from directories populated directly from session-model states (dirOfE), without keygen or PQXDH. While Alice's session is unacknowledged, send wraps the SignalMessage in 3 ‖ PreKeySignalMessage (smPreKeyWire, smSendWire), and switches to 2 ‖ SignalMessage after her first receive.DEV-CLI1 (libsignal): the SM game delivers the inner SignalMessage of a pre-key message. ACD19 has no pre-key layer, and on SM directories Bob holds no pre-keys, so cliScheme extracts field 4 (the inner SignalMessage) from Alice's printed 3 ‖ PreKeySignalMessage and delivers 2 ‖ SignalMessage (DEV-M5). smSendModel specifies the CLI's full stdout including the wrapper. Effect: none on the security bound.DEV-CLI2 (libsignal): the SM spec covers recv of whisper messages only. smRecvInputs passes 2 ‖ c for all c shorter than 2^32 bytes (DEV-M1), matching every ciphertext delivered by cliScheme (DEV-CLI1); 3 ‖ … inputs are outside the SM spec. Effect: none.\n\n\n\nThe parties' directories\n\ndef partyAddressL51–52 ↗#The address of each party: name partyAddressName P (a hyphenated UUID), device 1 (partyDeviceId).\n\ndef textBytesL54–55 ↗#The bytes of a text file: the string's UTF-8 encoding.\n\n9 declarations not shown (lines 57–126): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.SignalSession.dirOfE, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smInputs, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smSendInputs, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smRecvInputs, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smWrites, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smPreKeyWire, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smSendWire, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smSendModel, CryptoVerification.SignalSession.smRecvModel\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/SmDir.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.SmDir/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___SmDir"}});